WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.429 --> 00:00:06.379
This year a grand technicolor film about showbiz
is a favorite for best picture at the Oscars.

00:00:06.379 --> 00:00:10.930
Yes, it was well regarded amongst critics
and audiences around the country.

00:00:10.930 --> 00:00:15.640
But is La La Land hands down the best film
of the year?

00:00:15.640 --> 00:00:21.540
Is it good enough to beat out films like Moonlight
that are widely considered more daring and

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:22.540
unique?

00:00:22.540 --> 00:00:23.890
History says yes.

00:00:23.890 --> 00:00:29.570
Because the oscar voting process favors mediocrity.

00:00:29.570 --> 00:00:33.960
Back in 2009 the the Academy switched from
a straight popular vote to instant runoff

00:00:33.960 --> 00:00:36.520
voting or preferential voting.

00:00:36.520 --> 00:00:41.290
The Academy wanted to better insure that the
film with the broadest support won.

00:00:41.290 --> 00:00:47.190
But the other side of that coin is that bold,
polarizing films get pushed to the side.

00:00:47.190 --> 00:00:52.010
At its most basic level, instant runoff voting
involves ranking a number of choices rather

00:00:52.010 --> 00:00:54.060
than choosing just one.

00:00:54.060 --> 00:00:56.790
Then the choice with the fewest votes is removed.

00:00:56.790 --> 00:01:00.030
And then those who voted for that candidate
have their votes counted according to their

00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:02.260
second-favorite candidate.

00:01:02.260 --> 00:01:05.970
Then the candidate that now has the fewest
votes is removed, and so on.

00:01:05.970 --> 00:01:10.890
It goes all the way until a candidate has
50% + 1 of the vote.

00:01:10.890 --> 00:01:15.040
This applies to both the nominations process
(although that does get a little weedy) and

00:01:15.040 --> 00:01:17.780
the process of selecting a best picture winner.

00:01:17.780 --> 00:01:22.439
So, how would instant runoff voting ultimately
play out in a real scenario?

00:01:22.439 --> 00:01:27.909
Let’s look at 2011 where the King’s Speech
beat out: 127 hours, The Fighter, Black Swan,

00:01:27.909 --> 00:01:33.479
Winter's Bone, True Grit, Inception, The Social
Network, and The Kids are Alright.

00:01:33.479 --> 00:01:37.850
All these films were probably 1st place picks
on a lot of ballots and dead last on others.

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:43.049
It’s very possible that the passionate fan
bases of each of these films all had the King’s

00:01:43.049 --> 00:01:44.720
speech ranked 2nd or 3rd.

00:01:44.720 --> 00:01:48.860
When their 1st place vote wasn’t enough
to stay in the game their 2nd place votes

00:01:48.860 --> 00:01:53.470
were counted and re-added to the mix, ultimately
allowing The King’s Speech to come from

00:01:53.470 --> 00:01:54.540
behind.

00:01:54.540 --> 00:01:58.990
Because the King’s Speech had the broadest
support rather than the most passionate support,

00:01:58.990 --> 00:02:02.320
it took home the prize.

00:02:02.320 --> 00:02:05.080
The new voting system seems to favor a certain
type of film.

00:02:05.080 --> 00:02:11.650
Todd: We’ve had instant runoff voting for
6 years and fully half of those films have

00:02:11.650 --> 00:02:12.999
been movies about the movies.

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:17.629
And I’d count the King’s Speech as being
adjacent to that.

00:02:17.629 --> 00:02:22.409
It’s a film about performance and elocution.

00:02:22.409 --> 00:02:24.879
Think Birdman, Argo, The Artist.

00:02:24.879 --> 00:02:31.689
The Academy is made of 6000 film industry
professionals who probably enjoy movies about

00:02:31.689 --> 00:02:32.959
themselves.

00:02:32.959 --> 00:02:38.159
They might not rank a film about showbiz as
number 1 but many might place it 2nd or third

00:02:38.159 --> 00:02:41.470
which is precisely where it's most dangerous.

00:02:41.470 --> 00:02:45.529
In 2005 before instant runoff voting was instituted,
Crash, won best picture.

00:02:45.529 --> 00:02:46.529
It’s a film people either despise or love.

00:02:46.529 --> 00:02:54.489
Todd: Crash is the worst best picture of all
time but there are people out there who really

00:02:54.489 --> 00:02:55.489
love that movie.

00:02:55.489 --> 00:03:02.449
I think we really want those movies that inspire
those extreme reactions one way or another.

00:03:02.449 --> 00:03:06.279
Sometimes the movie wins that you hate and
sometimes the movie that you love.

00:03:06.279 --> 00:03:10.199
I’d rather see that than the movie that
everyone was kind of okay with.

00:03:10.199 --> 00:03:14.370
In fact, Crash beat out a film that might
have easily have won in today's instant runoff

00:03:14.370 --> 00:03:18.370
system: A period film about entertainment
(the radio industry) directed by Hollywood

00:03:18.370 --> 00:03:21.799
royalty, George Clooney: Goodnight, and Good
Luck.

